{"prompt_hash": "e5054b517edc706c73baea40eda1341058e0a0fa19cb27d849f7d58ce72a5cf8", "text": "Mortgage Options Comparison for $500,000 Austin Home\n\nLoan and Location Details\nHome price is $500,000 with 20% down payment ($100,000), resulting in loan amount of $400,000 for all options. Calculations use standard mortgage formula M = P * (r(1+r)^n) / ((1+r)^n - 1), where P is principal, r is monthly rate, n is number of payments. Austin-area property taxes approximated at 1.8% annual rate (common for Travis County based on regional data), but excluded from principal+interest (P&I) figures per query focus. No PMI needed due to 20% down. ARM assumes rate jumps to 7.5% (5.5% + 2%) after year 5, with fixed 30-year term thereafter.\n\nMonthly Principal and Interest Payments\n| Option | Term | Initial Rate | Monthly P&I | Total Interest (Full Term) |\n|--------|------|--------------|-------------|----------------------------|\n| A: 30-year fixed | 30 years (360 payments) | 6.5% | $2,527 | $510,720 |\n| B: 15-year fixed | 15 years (180 payments) | 5.75% | $3,336 | $200,480 |\n| C: 5/1 ARM | 30 years (360 payments) | 5.5% (years 1-5), then 7.5% | $2,271 (initial), $2,798 (after year 5) | $570,124 |\n\nTotal Interest Cost Comparison\nOption A totals $510,720 interest over 30 years. Option B is lowest at $200,480 but requires higher monthly payment. Option C starts cheapest ($2,271/month for 5 years) but ends highest at $570,124 due to rate increase assumption, exceeding A by $59,404. Shorter-term B saves most long-term despite larger payments; ARM risks higher costs if rates rise further.\n\nMortgage Calculator Tools\nUse these for custom tweaks including taxes/insurance:\nOption A/B/C Simulator: https://www.bankrate.com/mortgages/mortgage-calculator/\nTexas-Specific Calculator: https://smartasset.com/mortgage/texas-mortgage-calculator\nAustin Realty Tool: https://www.austinrealestate.com/mortgage-calculator.php\n\nSources\nhttps://smartasset.com/mortgage/texas-mortgage-calcul