{"prompt_hash": "0fc9a94c8d8d4767ea4901f2445cd81ba0d4a3146a9277e4aaf721a2cb056205", "text": "# Tokyo 3-Day Itinerary: Anime, Temples & Ramen (May 10-12, 2025)\n\n## Planning Your Anime & Culture Deep Dive\n\nThis itinerary balances Akihabara's anime culture, traditional temples in Asakusa, and high-end ramen experiences across three days. The route minimizes backtracking using Tokyo's efficient train network, keeping daily costs under 15,000 JPY through strategic meal planning and free/low-cost attractions.\n\n## Context and Assumptions\n\nBudget breakdown assumes: Suica card (2,000 JPY initial), train passes (1,500 JPY/day average), temple/shrine entries (free-1,000 JPY each), high-end ramen (1,500-2,000 JPY per meal), anime shops/arcades (2,000-3,000 JPY discretionary), remaining budget for casual meals and minor attractions. May weather in Tokyo averages 18-25°C with occasional rain; comfortable walking clothes recommended. Ramen restaurants listed are popular but require advance research for current hours and reservation policies.\n\n## Day-by-Day Itinerary\n\nDAY 1: AKIHABARA & SHIBUYA (May 10)\n\nMorning (8:00 AM - 12:00 PM)\nStart at Akihabara Station. Explore anime/manga shops on Electric Town Street and Chuo-dori. Visit multi-story arcades (Sega, Round1) for rhythm games and claw machines. Browse vintage anime goods at smaller shops. Cost: 2,000-3,000 JPY for arcade games and light shopping.\n\nLunch (12:30 PM)\nIchiran Ramen (Akihabara branch) - high-quality tonkotsu ramen, individual booths, approximately 1,000-1,200 JPY. Known for consistency and rich broth.\n\nAfternoon (2:00 PM - 5:00 PM)\nTake Ginza Line from Akihabara to Shibuya (15 minutes, 200 JPY). Cross Shibuya Scramble, explore Center Gai shopping street. Visit Shibuya Sky observation deck or Magnet by Shibuya 109 for shopping. Cost: 2,000-3,000 JPY (optional paid attractions).\n\nDinner (6:00 PM)\nIppudo Ramen (Shibuya) - tonkotsu or miso options, approximately 950-1,200 JPY. High-volume chain with reliable quality.\n\nEvening\nReturn to accommodation. Daily total: approximately 7,000-10,000 JPY.\n\nDAY 2: ASAKUSA & TRADITIONAL TEMPLES (May 11)\n\nMorning (8:00 AM - 12:00 PM)\nTake train to Asakusa Station. Visit Senso-ji Temple (free entry) and walk through Nakamise shopping street for souvenirs. Explore nearby Asakusa-jinja Shrine (free). Wander traditional backstreets. Cost: minimal (free attractions, optional small purchases).\n\nLunch (12:30 PM)\nIchiran or Ippudo Asakusa branch - maintain ramen theme, approximately 1,000-1,200 JPY.\n\nAfternoon (2:00 PM - 5:00 PM)\nTake Ginza Line to Ueno (10 minutes, 200 JPY). Visit Ueno Park and Tokyo National Museum (1,000 JPY entry). Browse museum gift shop. Cost: 1,000-1,500 JPY.\n\nDinner (6:00 PM)\nOotoya or similar traditional Japanese restaurant near Ueno - set meals (teishoku) featuring local cuisine, approximately 1,500-2,000 JPY.\n\nEvening\nReturn to accommodation. Daily total: approximately 6,000-8,000 JPY.\n\nDAY 3: TOKYO SKYTREE & GINZA (May 12)\n\nMorning (8:00 AM - 11:00 AM)\nTake Asakusa Line from Ueno or nearby station to Tokyo Skytree Station (5-10 minutes). Visit Tokyo Skytree Tembo Deck for 360-degree city views (2,100 JPY entry). Cost: 2,100 JPY.\n\nLate Morning/Lunch (11:30 AM - 1:00 PM)\nIchiran or Ramen Yokocho (small ramen alley near Skytree) - high-end tonkotsu or specialty broths, approximately 1,200-1,500 JPY.\n\nAfternoon (2:00 PM - 5:00 PM)\nTake train to Ginza Station (Asakusa Line, 15 minutes, 200 JPY). Explore Ginza Six shopping complex and luxury retail streets. Visit Ginza Sony Park or Mitsubishi Ichigokan Museum if energy permits (1,000-1,500 JPY optional). Cost: 1,000-3,000 JPY (discretionary shopping/museum).\n\nDinner (6:00 PM)\nHigh-end ramen experience: Nabezo or similar premium ramen restaurant in Ginza area - specialty broths and premium toppings, approximately 1,800-2,200 JPY. Alternatively, conveyor belt sushi near Ginza Station (1,500-2,000 JPY).\n\nEvening\nReturn to accommodation or explore Ginza nightlife within budget. Daily total: approximately 8,000-10,000 JPY.\n\n## Budget Summary Table\n\nDay | Category | Estimated Cost (JPY)\n---|---|---\nDay 1 | Transport + Food + Activities | 7,000-10,000\nDay 2 | Transport + Food + Activities | 6,000-8,000\nDay 3 | Transport + Food + Activities | 8,000-10,000\nTotal (3 days) | All categories | 21,000-28,000\n\nNote: Budget assumes single Suica card purchase (2,000 JPY, reusable). Actual daily costs depend on shopping purchases and optional paid attractions. Staying under 15,000 JPY per day is achievable by minimizing discretionary shopping and selecting mid-range ramen venues.\n\n## Quick Actions\n\nResearch current ramen restaurant hours and reservation policies for May 2025 via Tabelog (Japanese restaurant rating site) or Google Maps reviews.\n\nConfirm Tokyo Skytree entry prices and any discount passes available closer to travel dates.\n\nDownload transit app (Google Maps or Hyperdia) for real-time train schedules and Suica card compatibility.\n\nPurchase Suica card at Narita/Haneda Airport or any train station convenience store upon arrival.\n\n## Sources\n\nTokyo 3-Day Itinerary guides covering Akihabara, Asakusa, and Shibuya routing[1][2][3][4]\n\nTokyo Skytree visitor information and nearby attractions[5]\n\nTraditional Tokyo neighborhoods and temple access information[1][5]", "raw": {"id": "564ef587-fb7b-4f18-8721-f6c11ed892de", "model": "sonar-pro", "created": 1771343148, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 1129, "completion_tokens": 1404, "total_tokens": 2533, "search_context_size": "low", "cost": {"input_tokens_cost": 0.00339, "output_tokens_cost": 0.02106, "request_cost": 0.006, "total_cost": 0.03045}}, "citations": ["https://trulytokyo.com/tokyo-3-day-itinerary/", "https://janicerohrssen.com/blog/3-day-tokyo-itinerary", "https://thewanderbug.com/itinerary-3-days-tokyo/", "https://yourfriendthenomad.com/3-day-tokyo-itinerary/", "https://japanspecialist.com/w/the-ultimate-tokyo-3-d