eaching for a ladder to dust high shelves. Tourists milled about, admiring turret clock models inspired by the Orloj.[2] In a split second, Karel's foot slipped; the ladder teetered, sending the tray airborne toward a cluster of porcelain-faced clocks below. Shards seemed inevitable—ruin for František's livelihood.\n\nHis hand dove into his vest. Fingers closed on the watch. He pressed the hidden crown. Time halted. Dust motes hung mid-air like stars arrested in orbit. The tray floated, inches from disaster; Karel's eyes widened in perpetual surprise. Five seconds—an eternity in stillness. František sidestepped, plucked the tray from suspension, steadied the ladder, and repositioned Karel safely. With a click, time resumed. The ladder wobbled innocently; the tray settled harmlessly. Karel blinked, muttering, \"Slipped again, master?\" František smiled faintly, heart pounding. \"Careful, boy. Time waits for no one.\"\n\nThe Secret's Shadow\nWord of the watch spread in whispers among horologists, but František guarded it fiercely. He tested it sparingly: once to savor a stolen glance at a passing noblewoman, her parasol twirling eternally; another to evade a pickpocket in Staroměstské náměstí. Yet power corrupted subtly. Five seconds blurred into temptation—delaying a creditor's knock, glimpsing rivals' designs. By winter's end, the watch's glow dimmed, its hand inching past XII. František dismantled it one stormy night, gears dissolving into inert metal. \"Some mechanisms,\" he murmured, \"defy their makers.\"\n\nIn his final years, he crafted replicas of the Orloj for tourists, weaving the tale as folklore. Prague's clocks ticked on, oblivious, as František Novák faded into legend—a man who held time, if only briefly, in his palm.\n\nPrague Clockmaker Extensions\n- Expand to novella: Add Habsburg intrigue and Orloj sabotage plot.\n- Visual inspirations: Sketches of 1895 workshops or the magical watch.\n\nSources\n- https://www.orloj.eu/en/orloj_historie.htm[1]\n- ht