{"prompt_hash": "e426258a314012dc81f4ac043e1592abf3fc72010bd7e55216960bdc6e9db63d", "text": "# Family-Friendly Tokyo 5-Day Itinerary: Anime, Tech & Kid Museums (June 10-15, 2025)\n\n## Overview: Summer Tokyo for Families with School-Age Children\n\nThis itinerary balances interactive technology experiences, anime-related attractions, and museum visits suitable for 8-10 year olds during early summer (June). The plan assumes moderate walking tolerance for children, access to Tokyo's efficient train system, and pre-booking of popular attractions. Daily costs estimate USD 250-400 per family including meals, transit, and attractions.\n\n## Context and Assumptions\n\nThe itinerary assumes:\n- Family staying in a central location (Shinjuku, Shibuya, or Ueno recommended for train access)\n- Children comfortable with 2-3 hours of walking daily with rest breaks\n- Willingness to book popular attractions 1-2 weeks in advance\n- Use of Suica/Pasmo cards for transit efficiency\n- Early June weather: warm (75-80°F), possible rain; plan indoor activities accordingly\n- Budget includes meals at casual/mid-range restaurants, not fine dining\n\n## Day-by-Day Itinerary with Costs & Neighborhoods\n\nDAY 1: MODERN TOKYO & ANIME CULTURE (Harajuku, Shibuya, Omotesando)\n\nMorning (8:00 AM - 12:00 PM):\nStart at Yoyogi Park near Harajuku station. Walk through the park to reach Meiji Jingu Shrine, where children can participate in the water purification ritual. The shrine grounds are peaceful and suitable for families.[1] Grab coffee at a nearby cafe.\n\nAfternoon (1:00 PM - 5:00 PM):\nHead to Takeshita Street in Harajuku, famous for youth culture and anime-related shops. Children aged 8-10 typically enjoy browsing anime merchandise, character goods, and quirky fashion stores. For lunch, cook okonomiyaki (Japanese savory pancakes) together at Sakura-tei, which offers an interactive dining experience.[1] Reserve ahead.\n\nLate Afternoon/Evening (5:00 PM - 8:30 PM):\nExplore Omotesando's upscale shopping district and visit Tokyu Plaza to see the famous mirrored escalator (visually striking for children). End the day at Nezu Museum in Aoyama, open until 5 PM, which features pre-modern art and beautiful gardens suitable for families.[1] For dinner, book tonkatsu at Butagumi or choose casual ramen nearby.\n\nEstimated Daily Cost: USD 280-350 (meals USD 80-100, attractions USD 100-120, transit USD 30-40, shopping variable)\n\nNeighborhood: Harajuku, Shibuya, Aoyama\n\n---\n\nDAY 2: TECHNOLOGY & INTERACTIVE ART (Odaiba, Ginza)\n\nMorning (8:00 AM - 12:00 PM):\nTake the train to Odaiba. Visit teamLab Planets, an immersive digital art installation specifically designed for interactive engagement.[1][6] Children can walk through light-based exhibits, digital gardens, and interactive installations. Book tickets in advance online. This is ideal for tech-focused families and typically captivates children aged 8-10 for 2-3 hours.\n\nLunch (12:30 PM - 1:30 PM):\nEat at Odaiba's waterfront dining area or nearby malls.\n\nAfternoon (2:00 PM - 5:00 PM):\nVisit Miraikan (National Museum of Science and Innovation), featuring hands-on exhibits, robotics demonstrations, and interactive technology displays.[6] Children can explore space exhibits, digital technology zones, and engineering concepts. The museum is designed for family engagement.\n\nEvening (5:30 PM - 8:30 PM):\nReturn to Ginza for dinner. Browse Ginza's shopping streets or visit the Ginza district's casual dining options. If energy permits, explore anime/manga shops in nearby Akihabara.\n\nEstimated Daily Cost: USD 300-400 (teamLab Planets tickets USD 100-130 per person, Miraikan USD 25-30 per person, meals USD 80-100, transit USD 30-40)\n\nNeighborhood: Odaiba, Ginza, Akihabara (evening)\n\n---\n\nDAY 3: AQUARIUMS & CULTURAL MUSEUMS (Asakusa, Ueno)\n\nMorning (8:00 AM - 12:00 PM):\nVisit Sumida Aquarium at Tokyo Skytree. This aquarium is highly recommended for families with children; it features beautiful displays, intimate exhibits (no large ocean animals), and a stunning goldfish collection.[3] The aquarium opens at 9 AM and is compact, making it suitable for younger children who tire easily.\n\nLunch (12:30 PM - 1:30 PM):\nExplore Asakusa neighborhood near Sumida Aquarium. Visit the famous Senso-ji Temple.[2] Try local eats including sweet potato desserts at Imo Pippi or ramen shops in the area.[3]\n\nAfternoon (2:00 PM - 5:00 PM):\nHead to Ueno Park and visit either the Tokyo National Museum or the National Museum of Nature & Science. Both feature exhibits engaging for school-age children.[5] The museums are affordable and offer diverse collections (art, natural history, science). Ueno Zoo is also nearby and free for children, with restaurants surrounding the area for easy lunch options.[3]\n\nEvening (5:30 PM - 8:30 PM):\nRest at hotel or explore Ueno's casual dining. Consider a light dinner near the park.\n\nEstimated Daily Cost: USD 250-320 (Sumida Aquarium USD 20-25 per person, museum entry USD 15-20 per person, meals USD 80-100, transit USD 30-40)\n\nNeighborhood: Asakusa, Ueno\n\n---\n\nDAY 4: ANIME & GAMING DISTRICT (Akihabara, Shinjuku)\n\nMorning (8:00 AM - 12:00 PM):\nSpend the morning in Akihabara, Tokyo's anime, manga, and gaming hub. Visit multi-story anime shops (Animate, Mandarake), gaming arcades, and character goods stores. Children aged 8-10 typically enjoy browsing anime merchandise, playing arcade games, and exploring the district's unique culture. Budget time for arcade games (USD 1-3 per game).\n\nLunch (12:30 PM - 1:30 PM):\nEat at casual ramen shops, conveyor-belt sushi, or themed cafes in Akihabara.\n\nAfternoon (2:00 PM - 5:00 PM):\nTravel to Shinjuku. Visit the Imperial Palace East Garden or take a leisurely walk through Shinjuku's parks.[2] Alternatively, explore Shinjuku's shopping districts, which have toy stores and anime-related shops.\n\nEvening (5:30 PM - 8:30 PM):\nEnjoy dinner in Shinjuku. The district has numerous family-friendly restaurants ranging from casual to mid-range. Consider a show or entertainment option if children have energy.\n\nEstimated Daily Cost: USD 280-350 (arcade games/shopping USD 60-100, meals USD 80-100, transit USD 30-40)\n\nNeighborhood: Akihabara, Shinjuku\n\n---\n\nDAY 5: FISH MARKET & CULTURAL SITES (Tsukiji, Imperial Palace Area)\n\nMorning (7:00 AM - 11:00 AM):\nRise early to visit Tsukiji Outer Market. Experience the bustling fish market, sample fresh sushi breakfast at Sushizanmai or market stalls, and browse food and cookware shops.[1] This is an authentic Tokyo experience and children often enjoy the sensory experience of the market.\n\nLate Morning/Afternoon (11:30 AM - 4:00 PM):\nVisit the Imperial Palace East Garden, which offers peaceful walks, traditional architecture, and beautiful gardens suitable for families.[2] Alternatively, if children prefer more interactive experiences, visit the Edo-Tokyo Museum (accessible from Asakusa or Tokyo Station), which features interactive exhibits about Tokyo's history.[2]\n\nLate Afternoon (4:00 PM - 6:00 PM):\nFinal shopping in Ginza or return to favorite neighborhoods for last-minute purchases.\n\nEvening (6:30 PM onwards):\nDinner near your hotel or at a favorite restaurant from earlier in the trip. Prepare for departure if leaving on the 15th.\n\nEstimated Daily Cost: USD 220-300 (market breakfast USD 30-50, museum/garden entry USD 15-25 per person, meals USD 80-100, transit USD 30-40, shopping variable)\n\nNeighborhood: Tsukiji, Imperial Palace Area, Ginza\n\n---\n\n## Cost Summary & Budget Framework\n\nDaily Breakdown (per family of 4):\n\nDay 1 (Modern Tokyo): USD 280-350\nDay 2 (Technology): USD 300-400\nDay 3 (Aquariums/Museums): USD 250-320\nDay 4 (Anime/Gaming): USD 280-350\nDay 5 (Markets/Culture): USD 220-300\n\nTotal 5-Day Estimate: USD 1,330-1,720 (excluding accommodation, flights, and major shopping)\n\nThis includes:\n- Attraction entry fees (USD 15-130 per person per day)\n- Meals at casual/mid-range restaurants (USD 80-100 daily)\n- Local transit via Suica/Pasmo card (USD 30-40 daily)\n- Incidental shopping and arcade games (variable)\n\nNot included: Hotel (USD 100-250 per night typical for family rooms), flights, travel insurance\n\n---\n\n## Quick Actions for Planning\n\n1. Book teamLab Planets and Miraikan tickets 1-2 weeks in advance via official websites\n2. Reserve restaurant tables (Sakura-tei, Butagumi) through hotel concierge or Tabelog\n3. Purchase Suica/Pasmo cards upon arrival for seamless transit\n4. Download Google Maps and Hyperdia app for train navigation\n5. Check Tokyo weather forecast mid-May for rain gear planning\n6. Confirm museum hours and closures (many close Mondays or specific days)\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\nOfficial Tokyo Tourism: https://www.gotokyo.org/en/\n\nteamLab Planets Tokyo: https://www.teamlab.art/\n\nMiraikan (National Museum of Science and Innovation): https://www.miraikan.jst.go.jp/en/\n\nSumida Aquarium: https://www.sumida-aquarium.com/\n\n[1][2][3][4][6] - Travel itinerary sources provided in search results", "raw": {"id": "b875e5aa-8122-4432-9620-d6589c6a685f", "model": "sonar-pro", "created": 1771343510, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 1132, "completion_tokens": 2207, "total_tokens": 3339, "search_context_size": "low", "cost": {"input_tokens_cost": 0.0034, "output_tokens_cost": 0.03311, "request_cost": 0.006, "total_cost": 0.0425}}, "citations": ["https://www.bontraveler.com/tokyo-itinerary/", "https://trulytokyo.com/tokyo-5-day-itinerary/", "https://www.mochimommy.com/tokyo-itinerary-with-kids/", "https://girleatworld.net/tokyo-itinerary-first-time-visitor/", "https://www.localpassportfamily.com/2025/06/our-one-week-japan-itinerary-things-to-see-in-japan-with-kids.html", "https://www.klook.com/en-US/blog/5d4n-travel-itinerary-tokyo-japan/", "https://www.gotokyo.org/en/story/walks-and-tours/tokyo-itinerary/index.html", "https://treehousedad.com/tokyo-with-kids/"], "search_results": [{"title": "The Ultimate Tokyo 5-Day I