{"prompt_hash": "50d9a00646f50c127973b63c8c6ea9602f18857c6ae60623537e602b67ce8299", "text": "# October Everest Base Camp Trek: Complete Packing List\n\n## Overview: Temperature Layering Strategy for October Conditions\n\nOctober offers ideal trekking conditions with daytime temperatures around 10-15°C and nighttime lows near -10°C. Success depends on a layering system that manages moisture while providing insulation. This packing list accounts for the dramatic temperature swings between hiking hours and stationary periods at teahouses.\n\n## Context and Assumptions\n\nThis list assumes a 10-day teahouse-based trek with porter support (duffel bag carried separately from your daypack). The temperature range -10°C to 15°C reflects typical October conditions at varying elevations. All clothing recommendations prioritize merino wool and synthetic materials over cotton, which retains moisture and causes rapid heat loss. Estimated total weight for duffel bag: 10-12 kg; daypack: 5-7 kg.\n\n## Complete Packing Categories for October EBC Trek\n\nCLOTHING\n\nBase Layers (worn against skin):\n- 2x long-sleeve merino wool tops (varying weights)\n- 2x merino wool thermal bottoms/leggings\n- 3-4x breathable underwear\n- 1x moisture-wicking short-sleeve shirt (backup)\n\nMid Layers (insulation):\n- 1x mid-weight fleece jacket or pullover\n- 1x lightweight down vest or jacket\n\nOuter Layers (protection):\n- 1x waterproof/windproof shell jacket (GORE-TEX recommended)\n- 1x heavy-duty down jacket rated to -20°C\n- 1x waterproof trekking pants\n- 1x lightweight trekking pants\n\nFootwear:\n- 1x broken-in waterproof hiking boots (mid-to-high cut, GORE-TEX)\n- 1x lightweight trainers or camp shoes for evenings\n- 10x hiking socks (merino wool preferred)\n- 2x liner socks (optional, for extra warmth)\n\nHead and Extremities:\n- 1x wide-brimmed sun hat (UV protection at 5,000m elevation)\n- 1x wool or fleece-lined beanie covering ears\n- 1x buff or neck gaiter (sun protection and dust filtration)\n- 1x pair thin liner gloves (moisture-wicking)\n- 1x pair heavy waterproof mittens or gloves\n- 1x pair sunglasses\n\nAdditional Clothing:\n- 2-3x spare underwear\n- 1x lightweight sweatshirt or fleece (optional)\n\nTREKKING GEAR\n\nBackpacks and Bags:\n- 1x duffel bag or large rucksack for porter (65 liters)\n- 1x daypack with rain cover (30-35 liters)\n- 1x waterproof backpack cover for daypack\n- Waterproof liners or dry sacks for organizing gear\n\nSleep System:\n- 1x sleeping bag rated to -18°C/0°F minimum\n- 1x sleeping bag liner (silk or fleece, adds 5°C warmth)\n- 1x travel pillow or pillowcase\n\nHiking Equipment:\n- 1x pair trekking poles (reduce knee impact by up to 25%)\n- 1x headlamp with extra batteries\n- 1x water bottle (minimum 1 liter) or hydration bladder\n- 1x lightweight water filter or purification tablets\n\nOptional but Recommended:\n- Hand warmers (chemical heat packs)\n- Gaiters (optional for October, essential Nov-Feb)\n- Crampons or chained spikes (not typically needed in October)\n- Sleeping bag stuff sack\n- Small locks for duffel bag and daypack\n- Spare bootlaces\n\nHYGIENE AND PERSONAL CARE\n\nBathing and Cleaning:\n- Large pack of baby wipes (primary washing method in cold)\n- Travel towel (quick-drying microfiber)\n- Hand sanitizer (use before meals)\n- Biodegradable soap\n\nToiletries:\n- Toothbrush and toothpaste\n- Deodorant (optional)\n- Sunscreen (high SPF for altitude UV exposure)\n- Lip balm with SPF\n- Moisturizer (dry mountain air)\n- Shampoo and conditioner (small bottles or solid bars)\n- Feminine hygiene products if needed\n\nMedications and Health:\n- Rehydration salts or electrolyte powder\n- Altitude sickness medication (consult doctor; acetazolamide common)\n- Pain reliever (ibuprofen)\n- Antihistamine\n- Blister treatment supplies\n- Adhesive bandages and medical tape\n- Antibiotic ointment\n- Anti-diarrheal medication\n- Cough drops (Khumbu Cough prevention)\n\nFIRST AID\n\nCore Medical Supplies:\n- Blis