{"prompt_hash": "a18b6961d1683eb344ba2d5444c33aeeba01e249f5e4718fd8ff7d762f43daaf", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"version\": \"v0.9\",\n    \"createSurface\": {\n      \"surfaceId\": \"cyberpunk-classics-surface\",\n      \"catalogId\": \"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json\",\n      \"theme\": {\n        \"primaryColor\": \"#00FF00\",\n        \"agentDisplayName\": \"CinemaGuide\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"version\": \"v0.9\",\n    \"updateComponents\": {\n      \"surfaceId\": \"cyberpunk-classics-surface\",\n      \"components\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"root\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"header_text\",\n            \"intro_text\",\n            \"movie_list\",\n            \"divider_actions\",\n            \"actions_header\",\n            \"actions_row\",\n            \"divider_sources\",\n            \"sources_header\",\n            \"source_link\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"header_text\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Classic Cyberpunk Cinema (1982-1999)\",\n          \"variant\": \"h2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"intro_text\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Here are five essential cyberpunk films from the genre's formative era, each exploring the core theme of 'high tech, low life' in a unique way.\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"movie_list\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"card_blade_runner\",\n            \"card_akira\",\n            \"card_gits\",\n            \"card_strange_days\",\n            \"card_matrix\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"card_blade_runner\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"content_blade_runner\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"content_blade_runner\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"title_blade_runner\",\n            \"director_blade_runner\",\n            \"desc_blade_runner\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"title_blade_runner\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Blade Runner\",\n          \"variant\": \"h3\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"director_blade_runner\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Director: Ridley Scott (1982)\",\n          \"variant\": \"caption\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"desc_blade_runner\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"High Tech, Low Life: This film defines the theme with its vision of 2019 Los Angeles. Towering neon skyscrapers and flying cars ('spinners') represent the high tech, while the perpetually rainy, grimy, and overcrowded streets show the low life. Advanced bio-engineered androids ('replicants') are hunted down in a society where technological marvels coexist with urban decay and a profound loss of humanity.\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"card_akira\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"content_akira\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"content_akira\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"title_akira\",\n            \"director_akira\",\n            \"desc_akira\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"title_akira\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Akira (Japanese)\",\n          \"variant\": \"h3\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"director_akira\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Director: Katsuhiro Otomo (1988)\",\n          \"variant\": \"caption\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"desc_akira\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"High Tech, Low Life: Set in the sprawling metropolis of Neo-Tokyo, the film showcases incredible technology, from futuristic motorcycles to secret government psychic energy projects. This is contrasted with a society on the verge of collapse, depicted through violent biker gangs, anti-government terrorists, corrupt politicians, and a disenfranchised youth rebelling in the decaying, neon-drenched urban landscape.\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"card_gits\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"content_gits\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"content_gits\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n            \"title_gits\",\n            \"director_gits\",\n            \"desc_gits\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"title_gits\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Ghost in the Shell (Japanese)\",\n          \"variant\": \"h3\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"director_gits\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"Director: Mamoru Oshii (1995)\",\n          \"variant\": \"caption\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"desc_gits\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"High Tech, Low Life: In a future where full-body cybernetic prosthetics are commonplace, the 'high tech' is the seamless integration of humanity and machine. The 'low life' is explored through the dark underbelly this creates: cyber-crime, 'ghost-hacking' (taking control of someone's mind), and a pervasive sense of identity loss as characters question their own humanity in a world where memories can be altered and bodies are disposable shells.\",\n          \"variant\": \"body\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"card_strange_days\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"content_strange_days\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"content_strange_days\",\n          \"component\": \"Column\",\n          \"children\": [\n       