{"prompt_hash": "828f898e5636ecccd6ddc8f78d4971c32e9716f5a66db2a4cd5e19c87beaf41a", "text": "Your Transatlantic Multi-City Flight Plan\nThis plan outlines the booking process and pre-purchase checks for a two-adult, multi-city trip in Economy class from New York (JFK) to London (LHR), then to Rome (FCO), and returning to New York (JFK) in May 2025. The focus is on Star Alliance carriers with at least one checked bag included and layovers under four hours.\n\nPlanning Context for U.S. Travelers\nThis checklist assumes both adult passengers are U.S. citizens traveling with U.S. passports. Visa and entry requirements can vary significantly based on nationality, so it is crucial to verify the rules for your specific citizenship and travel documents.\n\nPre-Booking Verification Checklist\nBefore committing to a purchase, review these critical details to avoid unexpected costs or travel disruptions.\n\nTravel Documents & Entry Requirements\n- Passport Validity: Confirm both passports are valid for at least six months beyond your return date (i.e., valid until at least late November 2025).\n- Visa and Entry Rules: As of now, U.S. citizens do not need a visa for short-term tourist stays in the UK or the Schengen Area (which includes Italy).\n- ETIAS Authorization: Check the official status of the European Travel Information and Authorisation System (ETIAS). This electronic travel authorization is expected to become mandatory for visa-exempt visitors to the Schengen Area. Verify the implementation date and application process on the official EU website.\n\nAirline, Fare & Baggage Policies\n- Carrier Confirmation: Ensure all flights are operated by Star Alliance member airlines (e.g., United, Lufthansa, Swiss, Air Canada, Turkish Airlines) to maintain alliance benefits.\n- Baggage Allowance: Carefully read the fare details to confirm that one checked bag per passenger is included for all three flight legs. Intra-European flights often have \"Light\" or \"Basic\" fares that ex