{"prompt_hash": "19a73a843ca6c86ff730aca7d56dee88638c3856b1f0d582e8581e954307a3a3", "text": "Resolving DaVinci Resolve Instability in the Fusion Page\n\nThis guide provides a structured checklist to diagnose and resolve the specific crash you are experiencing with the 'Surface Tracker' node in DaVinci Resolve 18.5 on your Windows 11 system. The steps are ordered from software-specific settings to broader system-level checks.\n\nSystem and Software Context\n\nThis response assumes you are running a stable, retail version of DaVinci Resolve 18.5 and not a beta release unless specified. It also presumes that your system components are not experiencing thermal throttling (overheating) and that any overclocks (including RAM XMP profiles) might be a potential source of instability.\n\nComprehensive Troubleshooting Checklist\n\nFollow these steps methodically to isolate the cause of the crash.\n\nStep 1: DaVinci Resolve Configuration\nThese checks focus on settings within Resolve that directly impact performance and stability, especially for GPU-intensive tasks in Fusion.\n\n*   GPU Processing Mode: Navigate to `DaVinci Resolve > Preferences > Memory and GPU`. Ensure 'GPU processing mode' is set to `CUDA` and that your NVIDIA RTX 4090 is the only GPU selected. Deselect the Intel integrated graphics if it is an option.\n*   Increase Fusion Memory Cache: In the same preferences window, navigate to the Fusion tab. Try increasing the `Fusion Memory Cache` limit significantly, as the Surface Tracker is highly memory-dependent.\n*   Use Optimized Media: The combination of 4K BRAW and the Surface Tracker is extremely demanding. In the Media or Edit page, right-click the clip, select `Generate Optimized Media`, and wait for it to complete. Perform the Fusion tracking on the optimized version of the clip.\n*   Project and Cache Settings: In `File > Project Settings > Master Settings`, ensure the `Timeline resolution` matches your source media. Under `Optimized Media and Render Cache`, confirm the cache format is set to a high-performance option like `DNxHR HQX`.\n*   Software Version: Check the Blackmagic Design support website for a newer point release of DaVinci Resolve 18.5 or consider updating to the latest stable version (e.g., 18.6), as your issue may be a resolved bug.\n\nStep 2: Media and Project Integrity\nThis section helps determine if the issue is with a specific file or project database rather than the software or system itself.\n\n*   Isolate the Media File: Try applying the Surface Tracker to a different 4K BRAW clip. If that works, the original clip may be corrupt. Also, try transcoding a short section of the problematic clip to a different format like ProRes 422 HQ and see if the tracker works on the transcoded file.\n*   Test in a New Project: Create a completely new, empty project. Import only the problematic BRAW clip and attempt the Surface Tracker operation. If this works, your original project database may have developed an issue.\n*   Verify BRAW Playback: Ensure the clip plays without issue in the official Blackmagic RAW Player, which can be downloaded separately. This confirms the file itself is not fundamentally damaged.\n\nStep 3: GPU Driver and System Configuration\nThese steps address potential conflicts between your powerful hardware and the operating system or driver settings.\n\n*   Experiment with GPU Drivers: While you have reinstalled 537.58, this specific version could have a regression. Using DDU again, perform a clean install of either the immediate previous NVIDIA Studio Driver or the absolute latest version available. A different version may have better stability.\n*   NVIDIA Control Panel Settings: Open the NVIDIA Control Panel, go to `Manage 3D Settings > Program Settings`. Select DaVinci Resolve (`resolve.exe`). Set the `Power management mode` to `Prefer maximum performance`. Reset any other custom settings to their defaults for testing.\n*   Disable Overclocking: If you have any CPU, GPU, or RAM overclocks (including XMP/EXPO profiles for RAM), disable them in your system's BIOS/UEFI. Run everything at JEDEC stock speeds to ensure hardware instability is not the cause.\n*   Windows Power Plan: Go to `Windows Settings > System > Power & battery` and set the power mode to `Best performance`. This ensures the CPU and other components are not being aggressively power-managed during intensive tasks.\n\nIcons:\n- Troubleshooting: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-icons@1.11.3/icons/gear-wide-connected.svg\n- GPU: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-icons@1.11.3/icons/gpu-card.svg\n- System Check: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-icons@1.11.3/icons/windows.svg\n\nQuick Actions\n\nAction: [Button: Visit Blackmagic Design Support] https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/support/family/davinci-resolve-and-fusion\nAction: [Button: Download NVIDIA Studio Drivers] https://www.nvidia.com/download/index.aspx\nAction: [Button: Check DaVinci Resolve Forums] https://forum.blackmagicdesign.com/viewforum.php?f=21", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "Resolving DaVinci Resolve Instability in the Fusion Page\n\nThis guide provides a structured checklist to diagnose and resolve the specific crash you are experiencing with the 'Surface Tracker' node in DaVinci Resolve 18.5 on your Windows 11 system. The steps are ordered from software-specific settings to broader system-level checks.\n\nSystem and Software Context\n\nThis response assumes you are running a stable, retail version of DaVinci Resolve 18.5 and not a beta release unless specified. It also presumes that your system components are not experiencing thermal throttling (overheating) and that any overclocks (including RAM XMP profiles) might be a potential source of instability.\n\nComprehensive Troubleshooting Checklist\n\nFollow these steps methodically to isolate the cause of the crash.\n\nStep 1: DaVinci Resolve Configuration\nThese checks focus on settings within Resolve that directly impact per