ach other, building up a huge amount of stress. When the stress becomes too great, the plates suddenly break free and slip, releasing a massive amount of energy. We feel this sudden release of energy on the surface as an earthquake.\nReal-World Example: The San Andreas Fault in California is a famous transform boundary. Here, the Pacific Plate is grinding past the North American Plate, causing the many earthquakes that California experiences.\n\nImages\n\nTectonic Plates Map: https://loremflickr.com/1200/800/tectonic,map\nHimalayan Mountains: https://loremflickr.com/1200/800/himalayas\nMid-Atlantic Ridge Diagram: https://loremflickr.com/1200/800/seafloor,spreading\nSan Andreas Fault Aerial View: https://loremflickr.com/1200/800/san,andreas,fault\n\nSources\n\nUSGS: This Dynamic Earth: The Story of Plate Tectonics\nAction: [Button: Read More] https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/dynamic.html\n\nNASA Science: Plate Tectonics\nAction: [Button: Read More] https://science.nasa.gov/earth-science/solid-earth/plate-tectonics/"}], "role": "model"}, "finishReason": "STOP", "index": 0}], "usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1263, "candidatesTokenCount": 782, "totalTokenCount": 3381, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 1263}], "thoughtsTokenCount": 1336}, "modelVersion": "gemini-2.5-pro", "responseId": "mzivafXdBdn04-EPmMOY-QU"}}
{"prompt_hash": "ba068bff8496c506060ae3f5ddb62df7bae0f53e86119379230cf2220df57b4a", "text": "Handling Flexible Function Arguments in Python\n\nThe special syntax `*args` and `**kwargs` in Python function definitions are used to pass a variable number of arguments to a function. The names `args` and `kwargs` are a convention; the important parts are the single asterisk `*` for non-keyword arguments and the double asterisk `**` for keyword arguments.\n\nUnderstanding `*args` for Positional Arguments\n\nThe `*args` syntax allows a function to accept any number of positional (non-keyword) arguments. These arguments are collected into a tuple within the function. This is useful when you don't know in advance how many arguments might be passed.\n\nExample: Summing Numbers with `*args`\nThis function can sum any quantity of numbers you provide.\n\ndef sum_all_numbers(*args):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of all positional arguments passed to it.\n    \"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    print(f\"Arguments received as a tuple: {args}\")\n    for num in args:\n        total += num\n    return total\n\n# --- Usage ---\nprint(f\"Sum: {sum_all_numbers(1, 2, 3)}\")\n# Output:\n# Arguments received as a tuple: (1, 2, 3)\n# Sum: 6\n\nprint(f\"Sum: {sum_all_numbers(10, 20, 30, 40, 50)}\")\n# Output:\n# Arguments received as a tuple: (10, 20, 30, 40, 50)\n# Sum: 150\n\nUnderstanding `**kwargs` for Keyword Arguments\n\nThe `**kwargs` syntax allows a function to accept any number of keyword arguments (arguments passed with a name, like `name=\"Alice\"`). These arguments are collected into a dictionary, where the keys are the argument names and the values are the argument values.\n\nExample: Displaying User Info with `**kwargs`\nThis function can display any set of user attributes you provide as keyword arguments.\n\ndef display_user_profile(**kwargs):\n    \"\"\"\n    Prints all keyword arguments passed to it, representing user data.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(f\"Arguments received as a dictionary: {kwargs}\")\n    for key, value in kwargs.items():\n        print(f\"{key.capitalize()}: {value}\")\n\n# --- Usage ---\ndisplay_user_profile(name=\"Alice\", age=30, city=\"New York\")\n# Output:\n# Arguments received as a dictionary: {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 30, 'city': 'New York'}\n# Name: Alice\n# Age: 30\n# City: New York\n\ndisplay_user_profile(username=\"bob_b\", status=\"active\")\n# Output:\n# Arguments received as a dictionary: {'username': 'bob_b', 'status': 'active'}\n# Username: bob_b\n# Status: active\n\nCombining Standard, `*args`, and `**kwargs`\n\nYou can combine standard arguments, `*args`, and `**kwargs` in a single function definition. The required order is: standard arguments, then `*args`, then `**kwargs`.\n\nExample: A Combined Function\nThis function takes a required `title`, any number of positional `topics`, and any number of keyword `metadata` items.\n\ndef create_report(title, *topics, **metadata):\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates a report summary using a title, a list of topics, and metadata.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(f\"--- {title} ---\")\n    print(\"Topics Covered:\")\n    for topic in topics:\n        print(f\"- {topic}\")\n    print(\"\\nMetadata:\")\n    for key, value in metadata.items():\n        print(f\"- {key}: {value}\")\n\n# --- Usage ---\ncreate_report(\n    \"Q3 Financial Summary\",\n    \"Revenue\", \"Expenses\", \"Profit Margin\",\n    author=\"Jane Doe\",\n    date=\"2023-10-26\",\n    status=\"Draft\"\n)\n# Output:\n# --- Q3 Financial Summary ---\n# Topics Covered:\n# - Revenue\n# - Expenses\n# - Profit Margin\n#\n# Metadata:\n# - author: Jane Doe\n# - date: 2023-10-26\n# - status: Draft\n\nQuick Actions\n\nAction: [Button: Read Official Documentation] https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#arbitrary-argument-lists\n\nSources\n\nSource: Python 3 Documentation: The Python Tutorial - More on Defining Functions\nURL: https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#more-on-defining-functions", "raw": {"candidate