\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"No\",\n          \"weight\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_header\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"variant\": \"h3\",\n          \"text\": \"Critical Use Case: Asynchronous Loops\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_intro\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"This example demonstrates a common scenario in asynchronous programming where the choice of keyword is critical. The goal is to log the numbers 0, 1, and 2 after a short delay.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_problem_header\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"**Problem with `var`:**\\nBecause `var i` is function-scoped, there is only one `i` variable shared across all loop iterations. By the time the `setTimeout` callbacks execute, the loop has already finished, and the value of `i` is 3.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_problem_card\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"async_problem_text\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_problem_text\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"```javascript\\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(i); // Outputs: 3, 3, 3\\n  }, 100);\\n}\\n```\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_solution_header\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"**Solution with `let`:**\\nUsing `let` creates a new, block-scoped `i` for each iteration of the loop. Each `setTimeout` callback captures a different `i` with the value it had during that specific iteration, leading to the expected output.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_solution_card\",\n          \"component\": \"Card\",\n          \"child\": \"async_solution_text\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"async_solution_text\",\n          \"component\": \"Text\",\n          \"text\": \"`